{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ac142c7b24a2d095","type":"text","text":"# Idle Leakage Dynamics and Readily Available Leakage Removal in Tunable Coupler QPUs","x":-1160,"y":-560,"width":508,"height":155,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"147863a75aa026f6","type":"text","text":"How does it depend on the state of the second qubit?","x":-2848,"y":292,"width":250,"height":74,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5a7ef619263c0963","type":"text","text":"Show also for having excitation on the second site","x":-2848,"y":645,"width":250,"height":94,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a8aec5f18d234f5f","type":"text","text":"### The coupler can turn off the qubit interaction and allow leakage mobility $|200\\rangle \\rightarrow |002\\rangle$.  ","x":-2528,"y":-14,"width":320,"height":140,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"4c0aa86475598092","type":"text","text":"Why does this happen if effective coupling is zero?","x":-2493,"y":303,"width":250,"height":63,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"734863e282ede178","type":"text","text":"Show that while the qubit interaction is not happening, leakage interaction is!","x":-3215,"y":639,"width":250,"height":130,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cd3dfb8a2838f5bc","type":"text","text":"Does it really? ","x":-3214,"y":292,"width":250,"height":67,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"d51bc84319d70831","type":"text","text":"How fast is the interaction?","x":-3024,"y":450,"width":250,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"3098db2f85e08895","type":"text","text":"Under what conditions does it happen? When does it not?","x":-2160,"y":281,"width":250,"height":87,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"e266974ab4222dd8","type":"text","text":"Tunneling can also be relevant.","x":-2160,"y":489,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"8d4c2cc5592451c5","type":"text","text":"Typically the conditions for this are met. So what is the point?","x":-1802,"y":284,"width":257,"height":84,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"b7d38912544c66f6","type":"text","text":"Enables leakage removal possibilities!","x":-1798,"y":469,"width":250,"height":59,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"c64b6f67901e24e2","type":"text","text":"There can be leakage in the coupler, does it also move? Does it affect the leakage moving?","x":-1460,"y":281,"width":237,"height":120,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"f92adab936c57a61","type":"text","text":"Show time dynamics for $|010\\rangle$, $|110\\rangle$, $|210\\rangle$.","x":-1585,"y":739,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"b162fce2dea5f62f","type":"file","file":"Coupler LRU/PaperSchemeFigures/CouplerSingleExcPlot.png","x":-1660,"y":1054,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"7ef51744a66bf6cd","type":"text","text":"### We can use readily available hardware on tunable coupler QPUs for PASSIVE leakage removal.","x":-158,"y":4,"width":320,"height":140,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"1f14eb0b168e562b","type":"text","text":"How can we do it?","x":-500,"y":251,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"a867a4aa73877c50","type":"text","text":"We can do x-gate on the second transmon, then if there is leakage we know it will be on the second site after specific time. Then we can tune the second transmon close in resonance with a resonator for reset. Then repeat.","x":-500,"y":444,"width":250,"height":254,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"4f620fe356f8ca28","type":"text","text":"Wouldn't the x-gate disturb the other qubit?","x":-850,"y":940,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"fd7be35936fa5f04","type":"text","text":"In what conditions we can do this tuning for reset?","x":-440,"y":920,"width":240,"height":80,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ed927d6877fb5d74","type":"text","text":"How much leakage does it remove?","x":-80,"y":920,"width":258,"height":80,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"dc45897c73de6f23","type":"text","text":"It removes as much as leakage hops to the second site. It can be repeated for additional reset.","x":-72,"y":1161,"width":250,"height":132,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"103c59e74f6daea6","type":"text","text":"How fast can we repeat it? Or, how long does the reset take?","x":-120,"y":1420,"width":250,"height":98,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"05217f23d3cf48e8","type":"text","text":"There are crosstalk errors, but these happen anyways.","x":-975,"y":1246,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"44d9925690e1bd2e","type":"text","text":"Since the coupler is in the off-position, there is no coupling.","x":-625,"y":1244,"width":250,"height":98,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a5d31635472665e2","type":"text","text":"The total time is the sum of:\nx-gate time (14ns?)\n\nleakage hop time (20ns?)\n\nadiabatic tuning/pulse time (30ns total in iqm paper, but it doesnt apply straight here)\n\nrelaxation time (9ns iSWAP between resonators, as in coupler assisted leakage removal)\n\nadiabatic pulse back (just turn it off, should be already calculated in the previous 30ns)\n\nTotals roughly 60ns? I guess under 100ns is good estimate.","x":-250,"y":1680,"width":510,"height":366,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"22b378e0c33304b6","type":"text","text":"Okay, but what about the leakage in the coupler?","x":260,"y":920,"width":250,"height":61,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"8976c8d1622784a1","type":"text","text":"This does not remove leakage in the coupler, and leakage in the coupler is a big problem for this setup.","x":262,"y":1154,"width":250,"height":122,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"17db4cad17caff9e","type":"text","text":"With engineered dissipation, there are other passive methods.","x":-88,"y":508,"width":250,"height":127,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"90c931afdce1d763","type":"text","text":"Why should we do this over other methods?","x":620,"y":920,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"825f52c88eea628e","type":"text","text":"By the tunable coupler this does not disturb the qubit, so we can do passive leakage removal.","x":626,"y":1171,"width":250,"height":122,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"c5ffaa502004b784","type":"text","text":"We saw that the leakage does not move, so what about removing leakage from the coupler?","x":280,"y":221,"width":250,"height":120,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"397ac4b582c249da","type":"text","text":"You can add one more coupler in resonance to the first coupler, there will be leakage mobility between these two,  then you can reset this coupler since we assume it is not used.","x":343,"y":487,"width":250,"height":211,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"55b0e8cd5d8bff7d","type":"file","file":"Coupler LRU/PaperSchemeFigures/CouplerFilterPlot.png","x":-3280,"y":900,"width":381,"height":400},
		{"id":"c0f1985a0db70cfd","type":"text","text":"Derive analytical expression for $|200\\rangle \\rightarrow |002\\rangle$ and $|201\\rangle \\rightarrow |102\\rangle$. Also derive these when assuming $w_1 \\neq w_2$ . Also interesting that what is the maximum population that hops to the other transmon. This should be as a function of the detuning $\\Delta$ between the transmons, and also interesting to see it for the state $|110\\rangle$, if there the hopping is more sensitive to the detuning (so that there is a sweet stop for allowing leakage mobility but not excitation hopping).","x":-2528,"y":654,"width":333,"height":346,"color":"4"}
	],
